# Phase 2: KMS - Session 2 Corrections & Additional Depth

## Corrected understanding (important - overrides earlier assumptions)
- KMS authorization has THREE independent paths, not two: key policy explicit
  allow, OR a valid grant, OR (when key policy delegates via root principal)
  IAM policy allow. ANY ONE succeeding is sufficient.
- An explicit principal statement in the key policy (naming a specific user/role
  directly) is a COMPLETE, STANDALONE grant - it does NOT also require a matching
  identity-based (IAM) policy. This was verified via AWS's own documentation
  after an initial incorrect prediction was made and tested wrong.
- Root-delegation statement in key policy ("Principal": {"AWS": "...:root"})
  means "defer decision to IAM" - only in THIS specific case does IAM's
  decision actually govern access.

